### Architectural boundaries

Separate responsibilities:

* UI components render interface and handle presentation.

* Hooks coordinate UI state and lifecycle.

* Services perform data operations.

* Firebase infrastructure handles persistence and media storage.

* Cloud Functions enforce trusted server-side operations.

* Validation handles input constraints and schema consistency.

* Privacy logic controls audience eligibility.

* Media processing handles optimization and upload preparation.

Avoid putting Firestore queries, media upload logic, permissions and complicated business rules directly inside large React components.

### 4\. Vibes data architecture

Create or adapt a consistent data model that supports the full Part 1 experience and future expansion.

Do not create new collections until the existing schema has been inspected.

### 4.1 Vibe document

A Vibe should support the following logical fields:

```ts
interface Vibe {
  id: string;

  ownerId: string;

  type: "image" | "video" | "text";

  media?: {
    storagePath: string;
    downloadURL?: string;
    thumbnailPath?: string;
    thumbnailURL?: string;

    mimeType?: string;
    width?: number;
    height?: number;

    sizeBytes?: number;
    durationMs?: number;
  };

  content?: {
    text?: string;
    background?: string;
    textColor?: string;
    fontFamily?: string;
    textAlignment?: "left" | "center" | "right";
  };

  audience: {
    visibility:
      | "public"
      | "followers"
      | "connections"
      | "close_circle"
      | "selected"
      | "private";

    allowedUserIds?: string[];
    excludedUserIds?: string[];
  };

  status:
    | "draft"
    | "processing"
    | "published"
    | "failed"
    | "expired"
    | "deleted";

  createdAt: Timestamp;
  publishedAt?: Timestamp;
  expiresAt?: Timestamp;

  updatedAt: Timestamp;
}
```

This is a conceptual contract. Adapt field names, timestamp types and optional fields to Arvdoul's actual conventions.

Do not store unnecessary duplicated user profile data in every Vibe.

Do not treat client-supplied `ownerId`, timestamps or permissions as trusted authorization evidence.

### 4.2 Media storage

Use a predictable storage structure consistent with existing Arvdoul conventions.

Example:

```text
vibes/
  {userId}/
    {vibeId}/
      original
      optimized
      thumbnail
```

Requirements:

* Validate file types.

* Validate file sizes.

* Generate safe storage paths.

* Avoid publicly exposing private media.

* Avoid trusting arbitrary download URLs supplied by clients.

* Prevent users from overwriting other users' media.

* Clean up abandoned uploads.

* Delete media when its associated Vibe is permanently deleted.

* Handle interrupted uploads and retries.

* Avoid duplicate uploads when a request is retried.

For private content, prefer authenticated media access or appropriately scoped Firebase download-token handling. Do not assume an unguessable URL provides sufficient privacy.

### 4.3 Expiration

Default Vibe lifetime:

24 hours

Requirements:

* Every published Vibe receives a trusted expiration timestamp.

* The expiration timestamp is calculated by trusted backend logic.

* Expired Vibes must no longer appear in ordinary active feeds.

* Expired Vibes must not be playable through stale client caches.

* Archive behavior must be separated from active-feed visibility.

* Expiration must be enforced by queries and authorization logic, not only by hiding expired cards.

* Expired media cleanup must be handled safely and asynchronously.

Use scheduled backend cleanup or an existing compatible expiration mechanism.

Do not depend on a user's device clock to determine whether a Vibe is valid.

### 4.4 Data access efficiency

Avoid fetching the entire Vibes collection.

Implement:

* Paginated feed retrieval.

* Cursor-based pagination.

* Limited queries.

* Appropriate composite indexes.

* Batched reads where appropriate.

* Efficient profile lookups.

* Cached media metadata.

* Deduplicated requests.

* Controlled real-time subscriptions.

Avoid unbounded Firestore listeners.

