* Accessible controls.

### 9.3 Privacy enforcement

The backend must independently verify whether a viewer is permitted to access a Vibe.

Do not rely exclusively on frontend filtering.

Enforce:

* Owner authorization.

* Audience eligibility.

* Selected-user permissions.

* Close Circle membership.

* User blocking relationships.

* Account restrictions.

* Content deletion.

* Expiration.

* Private media access.

A user must not be able to bypass privacy by guessing a Firestore document ID or reusing an old media URL.

### 9.4 Privacy edge cases

Handle:

* Creator blocks viewer.

* Viewer blocks creator.

* Creator changes audience after publishing.

* User is removed from Close Circle.

* User account becomes restricted.

* Selected audience changes.

* Vibe expires while being viewed.

* Creator deletes the Vibe.

* Account becomes unavailable.

The next authorization check must apply the latest permissions.

### 10\. Publishing system

Publishing must be reliable, recoverable and safe.

### 10.1 Publishing workflow

Implement this sequence:

```mermaid

flowchart TD
    A[Create Vibe] --> B[Select Media or Text]
    B --> C[Edit Content]
    C --> D[Preview]
    D --> E[Select Audience]
    E --> F[Validate Content]
    F --> G[Prepare Upload]
    G --> H[Upload Media]
    H --> I[Verify Upload]
    I --> J[Trusted Publish Operation]
    J --> K[Published]
    K --> L[Refresh Vibes Feed]
    F -->|Invalid| M[Show Validation Errors]
    H -->|Failure| N[Retry or Resume]
    J -->|Failure| O[Recoverable Publishing State]
```

### 10.2 Validation

Validate:

* Authenticated user.

* Valid content type.

* Valid media.

* Media file size.

* Media duration.

* MIME type.

* Text length.

* Text formatting.

* Audience selection.

* Media ownership.

* Required fields.

* Content integrity.

Client-side validation improves UX, but trusted backend validation is mandatory.

### 10.3 Upload lifecycle

Support these states:

* Idle.

* Preparing.

* Uploading.

* Processing.

* Publishing.

* Published.

* Failed.

* Cancelled.

Provide:

* Upload progress.

* Retry.

* Cancellation where safe.

* Duplicate-submit prevention.

* Recoverable failure.

* Clear error messages.

### 10.4 Publishing consistency

Avoid creating published Vibes with missing media.

Avoid creating multiple Vibes because a user tapped Publish repeatedly.

Use a stable client-generated operation identifier or equivalent idempotency strategy.

Use trusted backend operations to establish authoritative ownership, publishing timestamps and expiration.

### 10.5 Post-publish behavior

After successful publication:

* Close or transition out of Studio.

* Update the user's Vibes entry.

* Refresh or invalidate relevant queries.

* Make the published Vibe available to its authorized audience.

* Show a clear success state.

* Preserve the user's editing state only when appropriate.

* Avoid requiring a full application reload.

A failed publish must not display a false success state.

### 11\. Draft management

Drafts are essential for reliable content creation.

### 11.1 Draft capabilities

Support:

* Save draft.

* Automatic draft saving.

* Resume editing.

* Delete draft.

* Preview draft.

* Preserve selected audience.

* Preserve editing state.

* Preserve text overlays.

* Preserve media references where available.

### 11.2 Offline-first behavior

When a user loses connectivity:

* Preserve their current editing session.

* Avoid losing selected media.

* Save supported draft metadata locally.

* Show offline status.

* Prevent publishing from appearing successful while offline.

* Resume supported uploads when connectivity returns.

Use IndexedDB or an existing persistent storage mechanism appropriate to the current application.

Do not store large media blobs in Firestore.

### 11.3 Draft recovery

Handle:

* Browser refresh.

* Application restart.

* Unexpected navigation.

* Failed upload.

* Storage quota exceeded.

* Expired local media references.

* Missing original files.

If original media is no longer accessible, explain the problem and allow the user to replace it.

Do not silently discard drafts.

### 12\. Vibe expiration, archive and management

### 12.1 Active Vibes

Creators must be able to:

* View their active Vibes.

* Open their own Vibes.

* Identify remaining active content.

* Delete individual Vibes.

* Access appropriate management controls.

### 12.2 Expiration

After the configured lifetime:

* Remove the Vibe from active discovery.

* Prevent new playback.

* Update its lifecycle state.

* Preserve archive data when enabled.

* Schedule media cleanup according to retention policy.

### 12.3 Archive

Provide a foundational archive system.

Users should be able to:

* View eligible expired Vibes.

* Open archived content.

* Delete archived content.

* Understand archive availability.

* Control whether future Vibes are retained.

Do not assume all users want automatic archival.

### 12.4 Highlights

