### 22\. Agent execution protocol

If Firebase AI Studio or its available coding agents support multiple agents, divide the work by responsibility.

Agents must not independently redesign the entire architecture or create competing implementations.

| Agent   | Responsibility                         | Required evidence                                |
| ------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Agent 1 | Repository inspection and architecture | File map, dependencies, existing implementations |
| Agent 2 | Vibes data architecture and Firebase   | Schemas, services, rules, indexes, tests         |
| Agent 3 | Feed and Viewer                        | Functional UI, navigation and playback tests     |
| Agent 4 | Studio and media processing            | Working editing, validation and media handling   |
| Agent 5 | Privacy and publishing                 | Authorization, upload and lifecycle tests        |
| Agent 6 | Integration and quality assurance      | Regression results, build and security evidence  |

If parallel agents are unavailable, execute the same responsibilities sequentially.

### Agent coordination rules

* One agreed architecture must govern all implementation.

* Each agent must inspect relevant existing code before changing it.

* Shared interfaces must be agreed upon before dependent modules are implemented.

* Avoid simultaneous edits to the same critical files.

* Each agent must report exact changed file paths.

* Each agent must identify reused components and services.

* Each agent must report tests actually executed.

* Integration failures must be resolved before dependent tasks are marked complete.

* No agent may declare another agent's work verified without inspecting it.

* The integration owner must independently verify the finished feature.

### 23\. Completion tracking and proof of work

Every task must have one of these statuses:

| Status      | Meaning                                                          |
| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| NOT_STARTED | No implementation has begun.                                     |
| IN_PROGRESS | Work is actively being performed.                                |
| IMPLEMENTED | Code has been written.                                           |
| INTEGRATED  | Connected to the intended application systems.                   |
| VERIFIED    | Acceptance criteria have passed with evidence.                   |
| BLOCKED     | Work cannot proceed because of a documented dependency or issue. |

Only `VERIFIED` counts as completed.

For each task, maintain:

```text
Task ID:
Task name:
Status:

Existing implementation inspected:
Files created:
Files modified:
Files reused:

Implementation details:
Integration points:

Tests executed:
Test results:

Security checks:
Performance checks:

Known issues:
Outstanding work:

Evidence:
Verification decision:
```

### Mandatory anti-false-completion rules

Agents must never:

* Tick a checkbox without implementing the functionality.

* Mark a component complete because it renders.

* Claim backend integration without verified backend operations.

* Claim security without testing authorization.

* Claim responsive support without testing different viewports.

* Claim offline support without testing actual offline behavior.

* Claim performance improvements without measurable evidence.

* Claim media upload works without completing an actual upload.

* Claim publishing works without verifying the resulting data.

* Claim expiration works without testing the expired-content access path.

* Hide failed tests.

* Replace failing tests with weaker assertions to achieve a passing result.

* Create placeholder services and call them production-ready.

* Duplicate existing architecture to simplify implementation.

Every `VERIFIED` task must have evidence that demonstrates its acceptance criteria.

